      "content": "Singapore courts place significant weight on the unforeseeability of the event when interpreting broad force majeure clauses, viewing it as a core test for whether the tenant was genuinely caught off guard. However, this test is not automatic; the tenant must also prove the event was insuppressible and caused substantial damage that prevented performance, which chaotic civil unrest or terrorism often struggles to meet without specific proof of damage to the premises. Ultimately, while randomness supports the argument, the tenant often仍需 prove that the event falls under the contract's intent to exclude liability, as common law defenses generally pale in weight against a strict contractual breach caused by own negligence or failure.",
